UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>This is White Nights, a poignant tale of love, loneliness, and dreams, written by Fyodor Dostoyevsky, translated by Tim Zengerink, narrated by Zeke Ring.</p>' WHERE slug = 'white-nights' AND anchor_key = 'opening credits';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The narrator of this story has no name, and that is fitting, for he has almost no life to attach a name to. He is twenty-six years old and has lived in Petersburg for eight years without making a single friend. Having no people to be intimate with, he has grown intimate with things and with strangers: he forms attachments to the houses along the streets, greeting them as acquaintances, and invents whole histories for the passers-by he will never address. He calls himself, without much apology and without much pride, a dreamer. His real existence being empty, he lives instead upon invented romances, and the rare moments in which something actually happens to him he carries home and relives in fantasy until they are worn thin. The immediate cause of his sadness at the opening is small and precise: everyone in Petersburg is departing for the summer villas, and he is left behind, with nowhere to go and no one to invite him. It is out of this particular loneliness — the city emptying, the dreamer remaining — that everything else proceeds.</p>
<p>After a joyful day spent walking in the countryside outside the city, he returns at ten at night along a deserted canal embankment, and there he sees a young woman leaning upon the railing and crying. He does not dare speak to her. She notices him and hurries away; and here chance intervenes, for a drunken man begins to follow her, and the narrator, who would never have found the courage on his own account, drives the man off with his walking stick. That act of protection is what gives him the right to walk beside her, and thus the whole acquaintance rests upon an accident.</p>
<p>He does not court her. He confesses. Immediately and completely, without being asked, he tells her that he is shy, that he has never in his life had a relationship with a woman, that he falls in love only with imagined ideals, and that he does not know how to speak to a living one. What is remarkable, and what turns the encounter from an incident into a story, is that she does not mock him. She answers with warmth. She tells him that she too has no one in whom to confide, and that she feels as though she has known him twenty years already. She agrees to meet him at the same spot at ten the following night — but she insists that she must be there in any case, for a private reason of her own, which she will not disclose. And she sets one condition upon their friendship: he must not fall in love with her.</p>
<p>He accepts the condition, which is the only way he could have been given the friendship at all. He cannot go home. He walks the whole night through, declaring to himself that in two minutes she has made him happy forever, and has perhaps reconciled him with himself. In that declaration the tension of the whole story is already set: the happiness of a dreamer built upon two minutes of borrowed reality, and the question, not yet asked aloud, of what will be left of him when the loan is called in.</p>
<p>On the second night she returns and insists that they proceed sensibly. Before anything else, she demands that he tell her his whole story, for she does not yet know him. He answers that he has no story, because he has lived utterly alone: he has seen people all his life and has never truly spoken to any of them. Since he cannot give her events, he gives her a description of himself instead, and he gives it as though he were describing a species rather than a man. He is a type, he says: a dreamer, a creature who hides in a dim Petersburg corner as a snail hides in its shell, and who drives away his acquaintances one by one until there is no one left to drive away. He tries to make her understand the peculiar wealth of that condition — that the dreamer''s joy is real, an inner richness fed by fantasy, so intense and so obedient to his wishes that ordinary life afterwards seems dull, lifeless and slow by comparison. But he does not present this as a good bargain. He warns that a day arrives when the dreamer would give away every dream he has ever had for one moment of real, imperfect life. And he confesses that for him the day has come: his fancy has worn itself out, he has begun to celebrate the anniversaries of feelings that never occurred and of events that never took place, and he is afraid of growing old with nothing real behind him, not even anything worth regretting.</p>
<p>He expects laughter. What he receives is tears. Nastenka — she gives him her name — weeps, and tells him that she recognizes herself in what he has said, because she felt precisely the same thing during the two years in which she sat pinned to her grandmother. She reveals that she lives with a blind grandmother who, two years earlier, pinned Nastenka''s dress to her own so that she could not run off. That single detail explains her: a girl kept literally fastened to an old woman''s side, whose only escape has been the same one he has taken.</p>
<p>He tells her that she has made him stop despising himself, and that these two evenings have given him the only real happiness he has known — and that he dreads, even while saying so, the moment when it will end. She will not hear of an ending. She vows that they will never part, and asks him to be to her like an elder brother, one who gives her warm advice. They clasp hands upon it, as though sealing a pact, and then, the pact being sealed, Nastenka begins to tell her own story.</p>
<p>She is seventeen and an orphan, raised by the blind grandmother who pinned her dress to her own to keep her from misbehaving. The house has a top floor which is let to lodgers, and at a certain point a new lodger arrived: a young man, pleasant to look at, about whom the grandmother at once warned Nastenka to get no ideas. The bond between them began not with a declaration but with an act of tact. The lodger came upon Nastenka pinned to the old woman, saw her humiliation, said nothing at all, bowed silently and withdrew. He afterwards lent her books — French novels, Walter Scott, Pushkin — and these replaced her childish daydreams with a fuller imaginative life, which is to say that he made her, in a smaller way, what the narrator already was.</p>
<p>Then he invited her to the theatre. She refused, because she would not deceive her grandmother; whereupon he took a box at The Barber of Seville for all three of them, so that the old woman might come too. Nastenka understood only later that the invitation had been a test of her character, and that she had passed it. After the opera season, however, he withdrew from them entirely. Nastenka grew restless and began to waste away, and concluded that what she had taken for love had been nothing but pity.</p>
<p>Then she learned that he was to leave for Moscow for a year. That knowledge broke her. She made up a bundle of her things and went to his room at night, and offered, in so many words, to go away with him. He did not take her. He refused because he was poor and had no right to bind her to himself; but he swore that if in a year''s time she still loved him, he would come back and marry her. They kept the promise secret from the grandmother, and Nastenka waited.</p>
<p>The year is now up. He has been back in Petersburg three days, and he has not come to the bench where they agreed to meet at ten o''clock. That is the private reason she would not disclose; that is why she was standing at the railing weeping when the narrator first saw her; and that is why she has been at the same place each night since.</p>
<p>The narrator, who has by this time begun to love her, does the thing that seals his own fate. He advises her to write. He dictates a letter for her — a letter which asserts her right to write without reproach and without pleading, neither accusing the man nor begging him — and Nastenka, laughing, produces the letter already written, sealed and carried about with her all the while. She entrusts it to the narrator to deliver, and asks him to bring any answer to the bench at ten the next night. So he becomes the agent of his rival''s return, and does it willingly. They laugh together over Rosina, part with &quot;Till tomorrow,&quot; and he is left standing in the street elated and wholly in love.</p>
<p>It is on the third night that the truth of his position becomes clear to him. He understands that all the warmth Nastenka has shown him — the vow never to part, the brotherhood, the tears — has been the overflow of a joy that was meant for another man, spilling onto whoever stood nearest. It was never love for him. She arrives radiant and early, certain that her lover will answer that night, and she tells the narrator, with perfect frankness and no cruelty at all, that she likes him precisely because he has not fallen in love with her, and that once she is married they will be friends closer than brother and sister.</p>
<p>The lover does not come. Footsteps approach along the embankment, raise their hopes and then carry someone else past; and Nastenka''s gaiety collapses into anxiety. The narrator tries, obliquely, to say what he feels, reaching for the image of a forgotten melody suddenly reawakening in a man who thought he had lost the capacity for it; but Nastenka guesses his meaning before he can arrive at it and hushes him. So he swallows it, and turns instead to comforting her: he invents reasons why the letter may have been delayed, and promises to go and ask for a reply at first light. Weeping, she compares the two men aloud. The narrator, she says, is the better man, kinder and more thoughtful — and yet she loves the other more. She feels, too, that he is sacrificing something for her sake, and thanks him for it, telling him that he is no longer the isolated dreamer he described himself as being. They part with her promise to come the day after tomorrow whatever happens, and with her question: they will always be together, will they not?</p>
<p>The fourth day is rainy, and she does not come. The narrator wanders to their bench, and then as far as her door, and turns back in shame at the last moment, and the day ends in the old loneliness. No reply to the letter has arrived either, and he concludes bitterly that the lovers must already be together.</p>
<p>But on the following night she is at the canal at nine, and he must tell her that there is no letter and that her lover has not come. It destroys her last hope. She collapses into sobbing, calling three days of complete silence cruel and inhuman, and tormenting herself above all with the shame of it — that she was the one who went to him first, that she carried her bundle to his room, that she begged to be loved. She refuses the narrator''s offer to go to the man in her name, or to write again for her; she declares that she no longer knows him, no longer loves him, and will forget him. Then, in her grief, she asks the narrator whether he could ever have abandoned a girl who had bared her whole soul to him — and that question breaks his restraint.</p>
<p>He confesses that he loves her. He apologizes in advance for the absurdity of it and for the wretchedness of the moment he has chosen. And he insists that his love would never be a burden to her: he would love her so completely that even if she went on loving the other man, she would feel nothing from him but warmth, and the knowledge that there was someone alive whose whole occupation was her happiness.</p>
<p>She accepts him. She says she will get over the other, because that man mocked her while the narrator wept beside her; that she loves the narrator now because he is the nobler of the two. She reframes the whole year of waiting as possibly a childish delusion, born of a life shut up with her grandmother, and concludes that the man was never worthy of her at all. She gives him her hand and promises that her gratitude will grow into a love worthy of his.</p>
<p>What follows is the happiest passage of the narrator''s life and the shortest. They spend the night in a delirium of practical planning. He will move the very next day into the empty room on the top floor and become Granny''s lodger; they will bring Granny, and Matrona, and Fekla; Nastenka will give lessons; they will go to the theatre. For an hour or two the dreamer has a future made of ordinary furniture and ordinary people, which is exactly what he told her, on the second night, he would exchange every dream to obtain.</p>
<p>Then, as he is walking her home, a figure appears in the street and calls her name. It is the lover. Nastenka tears herself out of the narrator''s arms and runs into his. Before she goes, she comes running back once more, to throw her arms round the narrator and kiss him warmly, in gratitude; and then she takes her lover''s hand and the two of them disappear into the night, leaving him standing where he stands, frozen and hollowed out.</p>
<p>In the morning the servant Matrona brings a letter, and he knows the handwriting at once. In it Nastenka begs his forgiveness. She admits that she deceived him and deceived herself equally, and that what passed between them was a dream. She tells him that she loves him still, and that she wishes she could love them both at once — echoing what she had said before, that she wished he had been the lodger. She explains why her heart went back so instantly: because it had never truly left, because it had always belonged to that man, and there was nothing in it for her to bring back to him. She announces that she is to be married next week, and she asks the narrator to remain her friend and her brother, and to come and meet them both.</p>
<p>Alone in his room, he has a vision of the years: Matrona grown old, the room and the house across the street darkened and decayed, the wallpaper faded — and himself, fifteen years on, unchanged and just as lonely as he is now. The dream of the top-floor room, of Granny and Fekla and the lessons and the theatre, is closed off, and what remains is precisely the fate he had described to Nastenka on the second night, when he confessed that he feared arriving at old age with nothing real behind him.</p>
<p>And yet the ending does not fall where bitterness would put it. He refuses to blame her. He will not darken her happiness with reproach, or lay any weight of guilt upon her, or let a single bitter thought cross her sky; he wishes her clear skies and an untroubled joy, and he wishes it sincerely, without irony and without the concealed hope that she may one day regret. The story closes on the question he puts to himself instead: whether one whole moment of pure happiness is not enough for the entire life of a man. He does not argue the point. He simply leaves it standing, and the shape of his answer is plain — that it is enough, that it must be made enough, because for him it is all there is.</p>
<p>What the story is built to carry is contained in that last exchange between wish and fact. The dreamer''s disease is that he has substituted imagination for living, and has grown rich inside and destitute outside; his cure, when it arrives, arrives in the person of a girl who is herself half a dreamer, made so by being pinned to an old woman''s dress. For four nights he is given the thing he had told her he would trade every fantasy to possess: a real person, imperfect, in real time, who needs him. But the reality is not his. Every warmth he receives is overflow, every service he performs — the dictated letter, the delivered envelope, the promise to go at daybreak for an answer — hastens the return of the man who will take her away. He is, throughout, the instrument of his own dispossession, and he acts as that instrument knowingly, out of kindness, which is the only thing in him that is not fantasy.</p>
<p>Nastenka does not betray him in any ordinary sense. She says nothing untrue at the moment she says it. She sets her condition honestly at the start, that he must not fall in love with her; she warns him fairly that she likes him because he has not; and when she accepts him it is in good faith, and she is undone by her own heart rather than by any calculation. That is what makes the ending land as it does. There is no villain to blame, and therefore no consolation of blame. There is only the fact that a heart that has always belonged elsewhere will return there the instant it is called, and a man left standing in a street at night with the taste of a grateful kiss and a future dissolved.</p>
<p>He had said, after two minutes of her company on the first night, that she had made him happy forever and had perhaps reconciled him with himself. The last page tests that sentence and, in its way, upholds it. He is not reconciled to his life, which he foresees will decay around him unchanged. But he is reconciled to her, and to the fact of having once been alive for a few nights, and he declines to spoil that fact by resenting it. A whole moment of happiness, offered against a lifetime — the dreamer, who has spent his years counting anniversaries of things that never happened, has finally acquired one that did.</p>' WHERE slug = 'white-nights' AND anchor_key = 'the book in brief';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<h3 id="the-poetic-heart-of-a-lonely-dreamer">The Poetic Heart of a Lonely Dreamer</h3>
<p>White Nights is one of Fyodor Dostoyevsky''s most lyrical and tender works, a short story that captures the fragile beauty of human connection and the aching poignancy of solitude. First published in 1848, early in Dostoyevsky''s career, it belongs to a period before his more mature philosophical and psychological novels such as Crime and Punishment or The Brothers Karamazov. Yet, despite its brevity and relative simplicity, White Nights contains emotional and thematic depths that have made it one of his most beloved and enduring stories.</p>
<p>Set in the magical twilight of a St. Petersburg summer, when the sun never fully sets and the nights are awash in a surreal silvery light, the story follows a solitary, unnamed narrator, a dreamer who wanders the empty streets at night. He lives more in his imagination than in the real world, finding comfort in fantasies and silent buildings, in poetry and ideas. But his quiet, repetitive life is interrupted when he meets Nastenka, a young woman weeping on a bench.</p>
<p>Over the course of four nights and one morning, they share a brief, intense intimacy that changes them both. The story is not just a romantic interlude. It is an exploration of the human need to be seen, to be heard, to be loved. It is about the yearning for connection in a world that often feels cold and distant. Dostoyevsky, who himself was prone to melancholy and isolation, gives voice here to a character who longs not for glory or wealth, but simply for a moment of understanding.</p>
<p>Through the dreamer''s confession, the reader is drawn into a soul that feels deeply and asks for very little, but finds that even a little can be everything. Unlike many of Dostoyevsky''s later characters, who are torn by guilt, ideology, and inner chaos, the narrator of White Nights is gentle, kind, and almost painfully innocent. He is a man untouched by cruelty, yet marked by the pain of being invisible.</p>
<p>His love for Nastenka is pure and unselfish, rooted not in desire but in a deep emotional bond. And that is what makes the story so moving. It is not about possession or fulfillment. It is about giving without expecting, loving without demanding, hoping even in the face of certain loss.</p>
<h3 id="light-shadows-and-the-ephemeral-nature-of-love">Light, Shadows, and the Ephemeral Nature of Love</h3>
<p>The setting of White Nights is more than just a backdrop.</p>
<p>It is a vital part of the story''s emotional atmosphere. The White Nights of St. Petersburg. Petersburg, those luminous summer evenings where night never fully arrives, mirror the dreamer''s emotional state, a space between darkness and light, between sleep and waking, between fantasy and reality. It is a time when ordinary life is suspended and something otherworldly seems possible. This luminous unreality is what makes the dreamer''s encounter with Nastenka feel so enchanted.</p>
<p>They meet not in daylight, where the world imposes its rules and expectations, but in this in-between space where hearts speak more freely. For four nights, they walk and talk, sharing their hopes and sorrows. The dreamer opens up in ways he never has before. He is not trying to impress or manipulate. He simply wants to be close to someone. Nastenka, too, is lonely and vulnerable. She tells him of her strict grandmother, her confined life, and the man she loves who promised to return but has not.</p>
<p>Their connection is real, but fragile. They understand one another, yet they are not meant to last. The dreamer falls in love with Nastenka, but she is still in love with someone else. When her former lover returns, she goes to him, and the dreamer is left alone once more. But Dostoyevsky does not present this as a tragedy. There is sadness, yes, but also beauty. The dreamer has had his moment.</p>
<p>He was seen. He was needed. He was loved, even if briefly. And for someone who has lived in the shadows, that is no small thing. His final words are not bitter, but full of gratitude. A whole moment of happiness. Is that too little for the whole of a man''s life? This line captures the spirit of White Nights. It is a story about fleeting joy, not because it is destined to vanish, but because its very ephemerality gives it meaning.</p>
<p>The dreamer is not destroyed by disappointment. He returns to his solitude, but something in him has changed. He has touched another soul. He has known the light. This emotional nuance is part of what makes White Nights resonate so powerfully. In a world where love is often portrayed as dramatic, possessive, or transactional, Dostoyevsky offers a vision of love that is quiet, selfless, and tender. A love that expects nothing and gives everything.</p>
<p>A love that, though brief, illuminates a life.</p>
<h3 id="the-dreamer-the-outsider-and-the-seeds-of-dostoyevskys-themes">The Dreamer, the Outsider, and the Seeds of Dostoyevsky''s Themes</h3>
<p>White Nights is not just a love story. It is a portrait of the dreamer as an outsider. The narrator lives on the margins of life, observing others, constructing imaginary relationships, and yearning for a reality he cannot enter. He is not mentally ill, nor is he a social rebel. He is simply alone, painfully aware of his isolation, and yet he maintains a hopeful, even childlike heart.</p>
<p>This figure of the dreamer would evolve in Dostoyevsky''s later works into more complex, conflicted characters. Raskolnikov, the underground man, Prince Mishkin, Ivan Karamazov, all share with the dreamer a sense of dislocation, a struggle between inner vision and outer reality. But in White Nights, the dreamer is still innocent. He has not yet been hardened by suffering or drawn into philosophical rebellion. He still believes in the possibility of love, kindness, and simple human connection.</p>
<p>What makes this story so valuable, especially within Dostoyevsky''s larger body of work, is its emotional purity. It shows the author in a more tender mode, unburdened by religious or political weight. There is no murder, no confession, no courtroom, no philosophical duel, just two people walking through twilight, trying to make sense of their hearts. And yet, the themes that would come to define Dostoyevsky''s writing are already present.</p>
<p>The search for meaning, the pain of being unseen, the dignity of suffering, the paradox of joy and sadness coexisting. Even the idea of redemption, not through ideology or punishment, but through human connection, is here in its earliest, gentlest form. The form of the story, first-person narration, framed as a confession or letter to the reader, also anticipates Dostoyevsky''s later narrative techniques. The reader becomes a witness, not just to events, but to emotion.</p>
<p>We are not told what to think or feel. We are invited to listen, to empathize, to imagine ourselves in the dreamer''s shoes. White Nights also has a universal quality that transcends time and culture. Anyone who has ever felt invisible, who has longed for connection, who has experienced a brief, unforgettable love, will find themselves in these pages. It is a story about moments, about how the smallest encounter can echo through a lifetime, about how the heart, even when broken, can remain soft.</p>
<p>As you begin reading White Nights, let yourself be drawn into its quiet world. Feel the stillness of the city, the glow of the sky, the hesitations and confessions of the characters. Let the story move slowly. It is not meant to shock or thrill. It is meant to make you feel gently, honestly, deeply. Welcome to White Nights, a tale for the lonely, the hopeful, the dreamers. A soft whisper in a sleepless night, a reminder that even the briefest connection can light up the soul.</p>' WHERE slug = 'white-nights' AND anchor_key = 'introduction';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>It was a beautiful night, the kind of night that only feels possible when we are young, dear reader. The sky was full of stars, so bright and endless that, as I looked at it, I couldn''t help but wonder how grumpy or moody people could live under such a sky. That''s a very youthful thought too, dear reader, so youthful. But may God grant that such thoughts enter your heart more often.</p>
<p>Speaking of grumpy and moody people, I can''t help but think about how I felt all day. From the moment I woke up a strange sadness weighed on me. I couldn''t shake the feeling that I was completely alone, that everyone was leaving me behind. Of course, you might ask, who is this everyone you''re talking about? After all, I had lived in Petersburg for almost eight years and barely knew anyone.</p>
<p>But what did I need acquaintances for? I already felt like I knew all of Petersburg. That''s why, when everyone in Petersburg seemed to pack their things and move to their summer homes, it felt like they were all leaving me. The thought of being left behind scared me, and for three whole days I wandered the city feeling down, unsure of what to do with myself. Whether I walked along Nevsky Prospekt, visited the gardens, or strolled by the river bank, I didn''t see a single familiar face.</p>
<p>Those people didn''t know me, of course, but I knew them. I knew them so well that it felt like I had studied their faces. Their happiness made me happy, and their gloom brought me down. I even felt as though I had formed a sort of bond with one old man I saw every single day at the same time on Fontanka. He had a serious, thoughtful expression, and he always mumbled to himself while waving his left hand.</p>
<p>In his right hand he carried a long, knobby stick with a gold handle. He even seemed to notice me and care about me in his own way. If I wasn''t at my usual spot on Fontanka, I was sure he felt disappointed. Over time we almost started nodding to each other in greeting, especially when we were both in good spirits. Just the other day, after missing each other for two days, we nearly tipped our hats as we passed, but we caught ourselves just in time and simply exchanged curious glances.</p>
<p>I know the buildings in Petersburg too. As I walk down the streets, it feels like the buildings lean forward, peeking out their windows to greet me. Some of them seem to say, Good morning. How are you? I''m doing well, thank you, and I''ll be getting a new floor added in May. Others might say, I''m being repainted tomorrow, or I narrowly escaped a fire and was so frightened.</p>
<p>I even have favorites among the buildings. Some of them feel like old friends. One building plans to undergo some renovations by the architect this summer, and I''ll make a point of visiting daily to ensure the work goes well. Heaven forbid anything goes wrong. But I''ll never forget what happened with a small, charming house that used to be a lovely shade of pink. It was such a delightful little brick house.</p>
<p>It stood proudly among its plain neighbors, and seeing it always lifted my spirits. Then one day last week I passed by, and it seemed to cry out to me, They''re painting me yellow! I was horrified. Those barbarians! They didn''t spare a single detail, not the columns, not the cornices. My poor little friend was as yellow as a canary. It made me feel sick. Even now I can''t bring myself to visit that house, now ruined by its awful new color.</p>
<p>So now you understand, dear reader, how I feel connected to all of Petersburg. I already mentioned that I had been feeling uneasy for three whole days before I finally realized what was bothering me. I felt restless on the streets. This person was gone. That one was gone. And what had happened to the other? Even at home I didn''t feel like myself. For two nights I racked my brain trying to figure out what was wrong in my little corner.</p>
<p>Why was it so uncomfortable all of a sudden? In my confusion I carefully inspected the dingy green walls, the ceiling covered in cobwebs that Matrona had allowed to flourish, and all my furniture. I checked every chair, wondering if the issue was with them, because if even one chair isn''t in the same spot as the day before, I feel unsettled. I even studied the window, but none of it helped.</p>
<p>Nothing felt better. I thought about calling Matrona to talk about the cobwebs and her general carelessness, but when I finally did, she just stared at me in silence, clearly baffled, and left without saying a word. The cobwebs, of course, are still hanging there, undisturbed. It wasn''t until this morning that I finally figured it out. Ah, everyone is leaving for their summer homes. Excuse the simplicity of my words, but I''m not in the mood for fancy language right now.</p>
<p>Everything and everyone in Petersburg had either left or was preparing to leave for the holidays. Every respectable-looking gentleman I saw taking a cab seemed to transform in my mind into a devoted head of the household on his way to join his family at a summer villa. Every passerby seemed to carry an air of transition, as if to say to the world, I''m only here for a little while longer, and soon I''ll be off to the summer villa.</p>
<p>If a window opened and a pair of delicate snow-white fingers tapped on the glass, before a pretty young woman leaned out to call to a flower-seller, I immediately imagined the flowers weren''t for her stuffy apartment, but for the countryside, where she would soon be moving. I became so caught up in my new obsession that I began to feel I could tell, just by looking at people, which summer villa they were heading to.</p>
<p>Those going to Kameny or Aptokarsky Islands or the Peterhof Road stood out with their polished manners, stylish summer outfits, and elegant carriages. Those bound for Pargolovo or more distant places had a calm and dignified air. Meanwhile, people heading to Krestovsky Island were unmistakable with their carefree, lively energy. If I happened to see a long line of carts carrying heaps of furniture—tables, chairs, sofas, and all sorts of household items—often with an old cook perched on top of it all, fiercely guarding her employer''s belongings, or boats loaded with goods slowly gliding along the Neva or Fontanka on their way to the Black River or the Islands, it felt as though these scenes were everywhere.</p>
<p>In my mind I multiplied these wagons and boats tenfold, a hundredfold. It seemed like the whole city was moving in caravans to summer villas. It felt as if Petersburg was on the verge of becoming a ghost town. I started to feel embarrassed, frustrated, and deeply sad that I had no place to go for the holidays and no reason to leave the city. I wanted to join every wagon, ride off with every dignified gentleman in a cab, but no one, not a single person, invited me.</p>
<p>It was as if they had all forgotten about me entirely, as if I didn''t belong to their world at all. I went on long walks, often so lost in thought that I forgot where I was until I suddenly found myself at the city gates. As soon as I realized where I was, I felt a wave of relief. I crossed the boundary and walked through fields and meadows.</p>
<p>I didn''t feel tired at all, only as though a heavy weight had been lifted from my heart. Everyone I passed looked at me with such kind expressions that it felt like they were silently greeting me. They all seemed so cheerful, as if something wonderful had just happened, and nearly all of them were smoking cigars. Their happiness made me feel a joy I hadn''t known before. It felt almost as if I had been transported to Italy.</p>
<p>So powerful was the effect of nature on someone like me, a half-sick city dweller, suffocating within the walls of the town. There is something incredibly moving about the nature around Petersburg. Especially in early spring, when it seems to use every ounce of strength it has. It bursts into bloom, covers itself in green leaves, and sprinkles itself with flowers, as if putting on its finest attire. It reminds me of a delicate, sickly girl, the kind you might look at with pity or tenderness, or perhaps not notice at all.</p>
<p>Then, out of nowhere, she becomes breathtakingly beautiful, almost as if by accident. Her sad, thoughtful eyes suddenly glow with fire, her pale cheeks flush with color, her features soften, and her face fills with passion and life. Her smile becomes radiant, her laughter sparkles, and her beauty overwhelms you. It''s impossible not to wonder what caused this transformation. What gave her such vitality, such life? What brought that glow to her face and made her movements so full of grace?</p>
<p>But then the moment vanishes. The next day you might see her again, but she''s back to the way she was before, quiet, pale, and distant, with a look of sorrow in her eyes. You can even sense regret or anguish in her expression, as if she''s mourning the brief, fleeting moment of beauty she allowed herself. And you feel sad too, heartbroken that her beauty disappeared so quickly, that it came and went like a passing dream.</p>
<p>You grieve because it felt like you didn''t even have enough time to fully appreciate it, to truly love her. And yet, as beautiful as that day was, the night that followed was even better. Here''s what happened. I returned to the city very late, and it was already ten o''clock by the time I was heading back to my lodging. My path took me along the canal embankment, a place where, at that hour, you never see a single person.</p>
<p>To be fair, I live in a very isolated part of the city. I walked along, humming a song. When I''m happy, I always hum to myself, like anyone who''s joyful but has no one to share that happiness with. Then, out of nowhere, something completely unexpected happened. Leaning against the canal railing stood a woman, her elbows resting on the rail, as she stared intently at the murky water below.</p>
<p>She wore a lovely yellow hat and a stylish black shawl. She''s young, and I bet she has dark hair, I thought. She didn''t seem to notice the sound of my footsteps or react in any way as I walked past her, my heart pounding loudly in my chest. How odd, I thought. She must be lost in her thoughts. Then, suddenly, I stopped in my tracks, frozen. I heard a soft, muffled sob.</p>
<p>Yes, there was no mistaking it. The woman was crying. A moment later, the quiet sobs became louder, one after another. My heart sank. Though I was usually nervous around women, this was different. It felt like an extraordinary moment. I turned back, took a step toward her, and was just about to say, Miss, but stopped myself, realizing how cliche it sounded, like something out of every Russian novel I''d ever read.</p>
<p>That thought alone made me hesitate. As I fumbled for the right words, she seemed to collect herself. She turned her head slightly, noticed me, started, and quickly looked down. Without a word, she moved past me and began walking along the embankment. Instinctively, I followed her. She must have realized it because she veered off the embankment, crossed the street, and continued along the pavement. I didn''t dare cross after her, my heart beating wildly like a trapped bird.</p>
<p>Then fate seemed to step in and offer me a chance. On the same side of the pavement where she was walking, a man suddenly appeared. He was wearing evening clothes and looked to be middle-aged, though his unsteady gait and the way he leaned against the wall made him seem less than dignified. The woman quickened her pace, walking with the nervous urgency you see in women who want to avoid being approached at night.</p>
<p>It seemed likely the staggering man wouldn''t follow her, except, unfortunately, he did. Without any warning, the man sprang forward and began chasing her at full speed. She ran like the wind, but he was catching up, getting closer and closer. She let out a sharp scream, and in that instant I silently thanked the sturdy walking stick I happened to be carrying in my right hand. In a heartbeat I crossed the street, and in another the drunken man seemed to understand the situation.</p>
<p>He noticed my stick, recognized the unspoken thread it carried, and immediately backed off without saying a word. Only when we were far down the road did I hear him muttering curses in our direction, but his voice was too faint to make out. Take my arm, I said to the woman. He won''t bother us any more. She hesitated for only a moment before linking her trembling arm with mine.</p>
<p>She was still shaking, overwhelmed by fear and excitement. Oh, drunken stranger, how I silently thanked you in that moment! I glanced at her out of the corner of my eye and saw that she was even more beautiful than I had guessed, dark-haired, just as I''d imagined. Her long, dark lashes were still wet with tears, though I couldn''t tell if they were from the terror she had just experienced or from whatever had made her cry earlier.</p>
<p>Yet a faint smile was beginning to form on her lips. She, too, stole a quick glance at me, her cheeks turning a soft pink before she lowered her gaze again. You see, I said gently, why did you push me away earlier? If I''d been there, this never would have happened. But I didn''t know you, she replied quietly. I thought you might have been like him. And now?</p>
<p>Do you think you know me better? Maybe a little, she said, still looking down. But why are you shaking? Oh, you''ve guessed it right away, I replied, thrilled that this girl was so clever. Intelligence always goes so well with beauty. Yes, from the very first look you figured me out completely. You''re right, I''m shy around women. I get nervous just like you did a moment ago when that man scared you.</p>
<p>To be honest, I''m a little nervous now. This feels like a dream. And I never imagined, even in my wildest dreams, that I''d ever have a real conversation with a woman. What? Really? Yes, really. If my arm is trembling, it''s because it has never been held by a small, lovely hand like yours before. I''ve never had much to do with women. You see, I''m always alone. I don''t even know how to talk to them properly.</p>
<p>In fact, I''m not sure if I''ve already said something foolish to you. Be honest with me. Please tell me if I have. I promise I won''t be offended. No, no, not at all. Quite the opposite. And if you really want me to be honest, I''ll tell you that women actually like that kind of nervousness. And to be even more honest, I like it too. I won''t send you away until I''ve made it home safely.</p>
<p>You''re going to make me lose all my shyness, I said, almost breathless with happiness. And once that happens, all my chances will be gone. Chances? What chances? That''s not a very nice thing to say. I''m sorry it just slipped out, but how can anyone not want to make a good impression at a moment like this? You mean to be liked, right? Yes, exactly. But please be kind to me.</p>
<p>Think about who I am. I''m twenty-six years old, and I''ve never known anyone like you. How can I possibly say the right things or be smooth and confident? Maybe you''ll understand better if I just tell you everything. I don''t know how to keep quiet when my heart is speaking, but never mind all that. Believe me, I''ve never been with a woman, not even once. No friendships, no relationships, nothing.</p>
<p>Every day I just dream about finally meeting someone. Oh, if only you knew how many times I''ve fallen in love in my dreams. Really? With who? With no one in particular, with an ideal, with a person I imagine in my dreams. I make up entire stories in my head. You don''t know me at all. Of course, I''ve met a few women before, but what kind of women were they?</p>
<p>They were all landladies, people like that, but I''m sure you''ll laugh if I tell you this. I''ve even thought about speaking to some elegant woman on the street a few times. Of course, only if she was alone. I imagined myself approaching her timidly and respectfully, explaining how lonely I am, begging her not to turn me away. I''d tell her that I don''t have any opportunities to meet women, and that it''s almost a moral duty for her not to reject such a desperate request.</p>
<p>I''d ask her to say just a few kind words to me, as if she were my sister. I wouldn''t ask for much, just two or three words of sympathy. She could laugh at me if she wanted to, but I''d hope she wouldn''t reject me outright. Even if we never saw each other again, just those few words would mean so much. But you''re laughing now, and that''s exactly why I''m telling you all this.</p>
<p>Don''t be upset. I''m only laughing at how you''re your own worst enemy. If you had tried, you might have succeeded, even if it had been on the street. Simplicity is best. No kind-hearted woman, unless she were either foolish or in a bad mood at the time, could turn you away without those two little words you''re so shy to ask for. But wait, what am I saying? Of course, she might think you''re crazy.</p>
<p>I was judging by myself. I know quite a bit about other people''s lives. Oh, thank you, I exclaimed. You don''t know what you''ve just done for me? I''m glad, I''m so glad. But tell me, how did you know I was the kind of woman who—well, someone you''d think worthy—of your attention and friendship? Not just a landlady, as you say? What made you decide to approach me? What made me?</p>
<p>Well, you were alone. That man was being far too rude. And it was night-time. You have to admit, it felt like my duty. No, no, I mean before, when you were on the other side of the canal. You know you were planning to come up to me. The other side? Honestly, I don''t know how to answer. I''m afraid to say. Do you know I''ve been happy today?</p>
<p>I walked around singing. I went out into the countryside. I''ve never felt moments like that before. You—perhaps I imagined it. Forgive me for saying this, but I thought you were crying, and I couldn''t bear it. It hurt my heart. Surely it''s okay that I was concerned for you. Surely there''s no harm in feeling a kind of brotherly compassion for someone? I''m sorry, I said compassion. But really, is it wrong for me to have felt such a strong urge to approach you?</p>
<p>Stop, that''s enough. Don''t say any more, she said, looking down and pressing my hand. It''s my fault for bringing it up. But I''m glad I wasn''t wrong about you. And now, here I am at home. I must turn down this street. It''s just a few steps from here. Goodbye, and thank you. Surely, surely you don''t mean that we''ll never see each other again? This can''t be the end, can it?</p>
<p>You see, she said, laughing. At first, you only wanted two words. And now—well, I won''t say anything. Maybe we''ll meet again. I''ll come here tomorrow, I said. Oh, forgive me. I''m already making demands. Yes, you''re not very patient. You''re almost insisting. Listen, please, listen, I interrupted her. Forgive me for saying this, but I have to tell you something else. I can''t stop myself from coming back here tomorrow.</p>
<p>I''m a dreamer. My real life is so empty that moments like this are rare treasures for me. I hold on to them and relive them in my dreams. I''ll be dreaming of you all night, all week, maybe all year. I''ll definitely come back here tomorrow, to this very spot, at the same time. And I''ll feel happy just remembering today. This place is already special to me. I have two or three places like this in Petersburg.</p>
<p>Once I even cried over memories in one of those places. Just like you. Who knows, maybe ten minutes ago you were crying over a memory, too. But forgive me. I''ve let myself ramble again. Perhaps you''ve been especially happy here once before. Very good, said the girl. Perhaps I will come here tomorrow, too, at ten o''clock. I see that I can''t forbid you. The fact is, I have to be here.</p>
<p>Don''t imagine that I''m making an appointment with you. I tell you beforehand that I have to be here on my own account. But— Well, I tell you straight out, I don''t mind if you do come. To begin with, something unpleasant might happen as it did today, but never mind that. In short, I should simply like to see you. To say two words to you. Only mind you must not think the worse of me now.</p>
<p>Don''t think I make appointments so lightly. I shouldn''t make it except that. But let that be my secret, only a compact beforehand. A compact? Speak, tell me, tell me all beforehand. I agree to anything. I am ready for anything, I cry delighted. I answer for myself. I will be obedient, respectful. You know me. It''s just because I do know you that I ask you to come tomorrow, said the girl, laughing.</p>
<p>I know you perfectly. But mind you will come on the condition in the first place. Only be good, do what I ask. You see, I speak frankly. You won''t fall in love with me. That''s impossible, I assure you. I am ready for friendship. Here''s my hand. But you mustn''t fall in love with me, I beg you. I swear, I cried, gripping her hand. Hush, don''t swear. I know you are ready to flare up like gunpowder.</p>
<p>Don''t think ill of me for saying so. If only you knew, I too have no one to whom I can say a word, whose advice I can ask. Of course, one does not look for an advisor in the street, but you are an exception. I know you as though we had been friends for twenty years. You won''t deceive me, will you? You will see. The only thing is, I don''t know how I am going to survive the next twenty-four hours.</p>
<p>Sleep soundly, good night, and remember that I have trusted you already. But you exclaimed so nicely just now, surely one can''t be held responsible for every feeling, even for brotherly sympathy. Do you know that was so nicely said, that the idea struck me at once, that I might confide in you? For God''s sake do, but about what? What is it? Wait till tomorrow. Meanwhile, let that be a secret.</p>
<p>So much the better for you. It will give it a faint flavor of romance. Perhaps I will tell you tomorrow, and perhaps not. I will talk to you a little more beforehand. We will get to know each other better. Oh, yes, I will tell you all about myself tomorrow. But what has happened? It is as though a miracle had befallen me. My God, where am I? Come tell me, aren''t you glad that you were not angry and did not drive me away at the first moment as any other woman would have done?</p>
<p>In two minutes you have made me happy forever. Yes, happy. Who knows, perhaps, you have reconciled me with myself, solved my doubts. Perhaps such moments come upon me. But there I will tell you all about it tomorrow. You shall know everything, everything. Very well, I consent. You shall begin. Agreed. Goodbye till tomorrow. Till tomorrow. And we parted. I walked about all night. I could not make up my mind to go home.</p>
