<p>No, no, no, please, don''t stop, go on, I won''t say a word. All right, then, I''ll continue. There is, Nastenka, a certain hour of the day that I love more than any other. It''s the hour when most people have finished their work or errands, when they''re hurrying home for dinner, to relax, to rest, or to think about the pleasant moments their free time might bring. At that hour, our hero—yes, allow me to speak about myself—in the third person, since it feels too embarrassing to tell it in the first person, would join the others heading home, but on his face, pale and a bit crumpled, there was a strange expression of happiness.</p>
<p>He wasn''t indifferent to the fading glow of the sunset over the cold Petersburg sky, though he didn''t consciously notice it, either. It was more like he saw it without realizing, as though he were distracted by something far more interesting. He was happy because until the next day he was free from the tedious responsibilities that weighed on him. He felt as joyful as a schoolboy let loose after class, ready to dive into games and harmless mischief.</p>
<p>If you had seen him then, Nastenka, you would have noticed how joy flickered across his face, already affecting his sensitive nerves and overly vivid imagination. He wasn''t thinking about dinner or the evening ahead. No, he was lost in his own private world. Look at him, Nastenka. He''s rich now, rich with his inner life. The golden light of the setting sun warms his heart and stirs a swarm of impressions.</p>
<p>He barely notices the road beneath his feet or the little details that might usually catch his attention. The goddess of fancy, if you''ve read Zhukovsky, Nastenka, has already begun spinning her threads, weaving patterns of magical, enchanting life. Who knows? Perhaps she has carried him far away, to some shimmering dreamland, leaving the ordinary world behind. Stop him now, ask him where he''s going, and he wouldn''t know. He''d probably blush with embarrassment and make up an excuse, anything to avoid admitting he was lost in his thoughts.</p>
<p>That''s why he jumps in surprise when a kind old lady stops him on the street to ask for directions. Irritated at being pulled back to reality, he strides off again, barely noticing how passers-by glance curiously at him. A little girl stepping aside to avoid him, giggles at his distracted smile and odd gestures. But even these interruptions, Nastenka, are caught by his imagination and spun into the whimsical tapestry of his thoughts.</p>
<p>When he finally returns to his quiet little den, his mind is overflowing with new impressions. Matrona, his sombre and dutiful housekeeper, clears away his dinner, which he barely remembers eating. The room grows dark. The colourful kingdom of his fantasies crumbles into silence, like a fading dream he can''t quite recall. Yet his heart aches with a strange, tender longing. Something stirs within him, teasing his imagination to build new dreams.</p>
<p>As the house grows still, his solitude feeds his thoughts. A book lies abandoned in his lap, forgotten after just a few pages. His mind has already begun crafting a new world, filled with fresh adventures and delicate emotions. What does he care about real life, Nastenka? To him it seems dull, lifeless, slow—nothing like the vivid, enchanting world of his dreams. And yet, Nastenka, even as he loses himself in these dreams, a day may come when he''d trade all of them for just one moment of real, imperfect life.</p>
<p>Perhaps he doesn''t foresee it yet, but one day the hour may strike when he would give anything to escape his illusions and grasp something real. But for now, he''s content to live as the artist of his own existence, creating a new life for himself, every hour. When I finished my heartfelt appeal, I paused, letting my words hang in the air, feeling as though my emotions had poured out too pathetically.</p>
