<p>They will dry, Nastenka. They always dry. Sit down, sit down, she said softly, guiding me back to the seat. Her hands trembled as she moved, and her voice carried the weight of her own turmoil. Oh, my God! she murmured, almost to herself. No, Nastenka, I cannot sit down, I replied, standing again almost immediately. I can''t stay here any longer. You shouldn''t have to see me again. But before I go, I must tell you everything.</p>
<p>I must unburden my heart before I leave. I want you to know that you would never have found out I loved you. I would have kept my secret hidden. I would never have troubled you with it, never let my selfishness burden you at such a moment. No, I wouldn''t have dared. But I couldn''t resist speaking now, Nastenka. You spoke of it yourself. You gave me the opening.</p>
<p>It''s your fault, not mine. You cannot drive me away from you, not now. No, no! she said quickly, trying to conceal her confusion as best as she could, though her voice betrayed her struggle. I don''t drive you away. No, I don''t. You don''t drive me away, I said, shaking my head. No, but I meant to run from you myself. I meant to leave, to vanish, to spare you this.</p>
