<p>Occasionally Nastenka would decide it was time to go home, saying it firmly, almost as though to remind herself of a forgotten duty. Yet even then something always held her back. I couldn''t bring myself to insist, and so, despite her resolve, we found ourselves delaying again and again. I would offer to walk her home and she''d agree, but somehow, instead of heading directly to her house, we would meander back toward the embankment and end up at our seat, as though drawn there by an invisible tether.</p>
<p>And every time we returned Nastenka would sigh deeply, and tears would glisten in her eyes once more. Those moments filled me with a quiet dread, as if the fragile spell we shared might break at any second. But then she would press my hand gently, reassuringly, urging me to talk, to laugh, to carry on as though nothing had changed. And so we walked on, as though the night might stretch endlessly before us.</p>
<p>At last Nastenka spoke with a mix of finality and reluctance. It really is time I went home, she said, her voice softer than usual. It must be very late by now. We have to stop being so childish. Yes, Nastenka, I replied, though my voice lacked the conviction of hers. But as for me, I won''t be going home. I know I won''t sleep tonight. I don''t think I''ll sleep either, she admitted with a faint smile.</p>
<p>But still, you must see me home. Of course, how could I not? This time, she said with a mischievous glint in her eye, we really will make it to the house, promise me. Honor bright, I said with a laugh, raising my hand as if taking an oath. Truly, no more turning back halfway? Truly, I swear it. Well, then, come along. Come along, I echoed, but then I paused, looking upward.</p>
<p>Nastenka, just look at the sky. Look how beautiful it is tonight. Tomorrow will be glorious. You can tell by that deep blue and the way the moon shines so brightly. Do you see that golden cloud drifting across it now? Look, look, no, it''s passed by. Isn''t it breathtaking? But Nastenka wasn''t looking at the sky. She stood silently beside me, her body unnaturally still. When I turned to her, I noticed how pale she had become, as though all the color had drained from her cheeks in an instant.</p>
<p>A moment later, she moved closer to me, pressing her trembling hand into mine. Her fingers were cold, and I felt an uneasy chill crawl up my spine. What''s wrong, Nastenka? I asked, my voice barely above a whisper. Before she could answer, a young man passed by us, walking briskly. He paused a few steps ahead, turned, and stared at us intently. My heart began to pound wildly in my chest.</p>
<p>Who is it, Nastenka? I asked again, my voice unsteady now. It''s him, she whispered, her voice so faint it was almost inaudible. Her trembling intensified, and she clung to me as though seeking protection, though I could feel her pulling away even as she leaned closer, and then I heard his voice. Nastenka! Nastenka! It''s you! The sound of his voice struck me like a thunderclap. At that moment she tore herself away from me with a cry that was almost a sob, and ran toward him.</p>
<p>It was as though she had been released from a spell, her movements filled with desperate joy. My heart sank as I stood frozen, watching her fling herself into his arms. But even as she embraced him, she turned back toward me, her face radiant with gratitude and something deeper, something I couldn''t name. Before I could react, she ran back to me, her arms flung wide and threw them around my neck.</p>
<p>She kissed me warmly, tenderly, a gesture of profound affection and thanks. Then, as swiftly as she had come, she let go, turned back to him, and took his hand. Without a word, she led him away, her steps filled with a kind of trembling eagerness. I stood rooted to the spot, unable to move, watching them walk away together. My chest felt hollow, as though something essential had been torn from me, leaving only an aching emptiness in its place.</p>
<p>Still I couldn''t bring myself to look away. At last their figures disappeared into the distance, swallowed by the shadows of the night. I stood there for what felt like an eternity, gazing after them, unable to shake the weight that had settled over me. The world around me seemed suddenly quieter, colder, as though it too had been abandoned.</p>' WHERE slug = 'white-nights' AND anchor_key = 'fourth night';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>My night gave way to the morning, though the new day brought no relief. The rain fell steadily, its monotonous tapping against the windowpane filling the room with a sense of dreariness. Outside the world was shrouded in grey, and inside the dim light seemed to make the walls press in closer. My head throbbed, and a faint dizziness clouded my thoughts. A dull fever seemed to creep through my limbs, draining what little strength I had left.</p>
<p>&quot;Sir, there''s a letter for you, Matrona said gently, bending toward me. &quot;The postman just delivered it. &quot;A letter? I exclaimed, the words escaping my lips before I could process them. &quot;From whom? I asked, sitting up sharply in my chair. &quot;I don''t know, sir, she replied calmly. &quot;You should check. It might say who it''s from. My hands trembled as I took the letter. I stared at the envelope for a moment, recognizing the handwriting.</p>
<p>My heart leapt, then sank under the weight of anticipation. It was from her. It was from Nastenka. Breaking the seal with unsteady fingers, I unfolded the letter. For a moment the words swam before my eyes, and then I began to read. &quot;Oh, forgive me. Please, forgive me. I am begging you on my knees to forgive me. I''ve deceived you, and I''ve deceived myself. Everything was a dream, an illusion.</p>
<p>My heart aches for you today. Please forgive me. Don''t blame me, because I haven''t changed how I feel about you. I told you I would love you, and I still do. I love you now more than I ever did. Oh, if only I could love you both at once! If only you were him! &quot;Oh, if only he were you, echoed through my mind as I read. Her words stirred memories of what she had said before, cutting deeper into my heart.</p>
<p>&quot;God knows what I would do for you now. I know you''re sad and heartbroken, and I know I''ve hurt you. But you also know that when someone truly loves, they forgive quickly. And you love me, don''t you? Thank you, thank you for that love. It will stay with me forever, like a sweet dream that lingers even after waking. I will always remember the moment you opened your heart to me so selflessly, like a brother, and took my broken heart into your care with such tenderness and kindness.</p>
<p>&quot;If you forgive me, I will carry the memory of your love in my heart forever, like an unshakable gratitude etched into my soul. I will honor that memory. I will stay true to it. I won''t betray it, nor will I betray my heart. It remains as constant as ever. It found its way back to him so easily, so quickly, because it always belonged to him.&quot; I paused, the letter trembling in my hands, and I swallowed hard before continuing.</p>
<p>The words blurred, but I pressed on. &quot;We''ll meet again. You''ll come to us. You won''t leave us. You''ll always be my friend, my brother. And when you see me, you''ll give me your hand, won''t you? Please say you will. Tell me you''ve forgiven me. Tell me you still love me, just as you did before. Oh, don''t stop loving me. Please don''t abandon me, because I love you so much in this moment.</p>
<p>I want to be worthy of your love, and I promise I''ll work to deserve it. My dear, next week I''m marrying him. He has come back to me, still in love, still thinking of me. You won''t be angry that I''m writing to you about him, will you? But I want to see you again, with him. I know you''ll like him. Please say you will. Forgive me, remember me, and love me always.</p>
<p>Your, Nastenka.&quot; I read the letter again and again, unable to stop myself. Each word felt like a wave crashing over me, and tears filled my eyes until they overflowed. Finally, the letter slipped from my hands, and I covered my face, overwhelmed by emotion. &quot;Dearie, dearie, Matrona called out from the doorway. &quot;What is it, Matrona? I asked, my voice breaking slightly. &quot;I''ve taken down all the cobwebs from the ceiling.</p>
<p>You could have a wedding here, or even throw a party if you wanted, she said cheerfully. I turned to look at Matrona. She was still sturdy and strong for her age, a lively woman who carried herself with youthful energy, yet in that moment I suddenly saw her differently. In my mind she aged before my eyes, her gaze dull, her face deeply lined, her figure stooped and frail.</p>
<p>I couldn''t explain why, but that vision seemed to fill the entire room. I imagined my surroundings mirroring her decay. The walls appeared to lose their colour, the floors seemed worn and faded, and the cobwebs she had proudly removed seemed to grow back thicker and heavier than before. I couldn''t shake the feeling that the room itself was growing old with her, taking on the weight of years. When I glanced out the window, even the house across the street seemed affected by time.</p>
<p>Its once bright facade now looked dim and weathered. The stucco on the grand columns was crumbling and flaking off, the cornices showed dark cracks, and the once vivid yellow walls had turned patchy and dull. Perhaps it was the sun. It had broken briefly through the clouds, casting a golden light, only to retreat again behind the veil of rain, leaving everything looking grey and lifeless once more. Or perhaps it was my own imagination, painting my future in bleak, sombre tones.</p>
<p>I saw myself in that room fifteen years from now, just as lonely, just as unchanged. The same Matrona would still be there, no wiser, no more lively than she was to-day. But to think I could ever resent you, Nastenka. How could I? That I could darken your happiness with bitterness, or weigh down your heart with guilt. That I could speak words of blame to disturb your peace, or spoil the joy you feel as you walk to the altar with him.</p>
<p>Never, never. I could never be so cruel. Instead I wish for you the clearest skies, for your laughter to remain bright and your smiles unclouded. May you never feel regret, may your joy remain untouchable, and may you be blessed for giving even one moment of pure happiness to someone who will always be grateful. A single moment of happiness, a brief spark of light in a dark life.</p>
<p>Isn''t that enough? Isn''t that worth an entire lifetime?</p>' WHERE slug = 'white-nights' AND anchor_key = 'morning';
